{"id": "live-393", "title": "First Care Therapy, LLC v. Congress Ave Boynton, LLC (Commercial Lease Default Damages Reversed)", "category": "lease-disputes", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-11-12", "jurisdiction": "District Court of Appeal of Florida, Fourth District", "state": "FL", "amount": "$85,782.80 damages award reversed and remanded for a properly noticed damages trial", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11060078/first-care-therapy-llc-and-loodhinssy-lafaille-paul-v-congress-ave/", "summary": "Florida's Fourth District Court of Appeal affirmed a Palm Beach County circuit court's default judgment holding tenant First Care Therapy, LLC and guarantor Loodjhinssy Lafaille-Paul liable for breaching a commercial lease and guaranty on medical office space in Boynton Beach, but reversed the $85,782.80 damages award, finding the trial court awarded unliquidated damages without the properly noticed damages trial Florida law requires. The case returns to the circuit court for a new trial limited to damages.", "significance": "Confirms that a clerk's default, standing alone, entitles a commercial landlord to liquidated damages only, and that any damages figure requiring the court to weigh affidavits or other evidence, such as reletting commissions or tenant-improvement costs, still requires a properly noticed evidentiary trial under Rule 1.440(d) even against a defendant who never appears. Landlords who rely on post-default affidavits of indebtedness to support summary judgment risk losing the damages award on appeal even after winning on liability.", "judge": "Alan O. Forst", "tags": ["commercial-lease", "guaranty", "default-judgment", "florida", "unliquidated-damages"]}
